<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neuschwanstein Castle</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1886">1886</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neuschwanstein Castle is a 19th-century palace built for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-ii-of-bavaria">Ludwig II of Bavaria</a> on a rugged hill above the village of Hohenschwangau near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fussen">Füssen</a>. Begun in 1869 as a romantic imitation of a medieval castle and inspired by the operas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>, it was still unfinished at the king's death in 1886. Opened to the public weeks later, it has become one of the most visited sights in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neuschwanstein-castle.jpg" alt="Neuschwanstein Castle" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Wolf, www.foto-tw.de · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Neuschwanstein_2013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Neuschwanstein stands in Schwangau in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, above the village of Hohenschwangau. Ludwig's father, King Maximilian II, bought the ruined castle of Schwanstein in 1832 and replaced it with the neo-Gothic Hohenschwangau Castle, finished in 1837, where Ludwig, born in 1845, spent much of his childhood. Above it stood the ruins of two medieval castles, which Ludwig sketched in his diary in 1859. When he became king in 1864 a new palace there became the first of his building projects; it was named New Hohenschwangau Castle and renamed Neuschwanstein only after his death.</p><p>The design drew on castle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a> and on Ludwig's enthusiasm for the operas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-wagner">Richard Wagner</a>. Two journeys in 1867 gave detailed inspiration: one in May to the reconstructed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wartburg">Wartburg</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eisenach">Eisenach</a>, the setting of Wagner's Tannhäuser, and one in July to the Château de Pierrefonds, which Eugène Viollet-le-Duc was turning into a historicist palace for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The medieval ruins were demolished in 1868 and the foundation stone was laid on 5 September 1869. Eduard Riedel drew the plans in the Neo-Romanesque style; Georg von Dollmann took over the civil works in 1874 and Julius Hofmann the project in 1884. The king moved into the Palas in 1884. Ludwig paid for the palace from his private means and by borrowing, not from public funds. Its cost in his lifetime was 6.2 million gold marks, almost twice the first estimate.</p><p>The palace was fitted with the latest technology of its time, among it a battery-powered bell system for servants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> lines, running warm water and toilets with automatic flushing. Murals pay homage to the legend of Lohengrin, the Swan Knight. If completed it would have had more than 200 rooms, but no more than about 15 rooms and halls were finished.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Ludwig II died in 1886 with the castle unfinished. The keep of 90 metres planned for the upper courtyard was never built, and work on other parts continued afterwards in simplified form. The palace was opened to the public shortly after his death. Because of its secluded position it survived both world wars. Until 1944 it served as a depot for art looted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the Nazi organisation known as the Einsatzstab Reichsleiter Rosenberg, and in April 1945 the SS considered blowing it up, a plan that was not carried out. Since 2025 it has been part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> covering the palaces of King Ludwig II. Neuschwanstein is a symbol of the Romantic era and inspired Disneyland's Sleeping Beauty Castle.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neuschwanstein Castle” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ludwig II made Munich the scene of Richard Wagner's Tristan und Isolde in 1865 and helped finance the festival theatre at Bayreuth, and his castles of <b>Neuschwanstein</b>, Linderhof and Herrenchiemsee became some of the most visited sights in Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
